<h2 id="bg-col-07">BG-COL-07: Keep chart and map scales independent</h2>

**Local requirement: MUST.** Applies to categorical chart identities and proposed quantitative map/chart scales.

Choosing a theme colour must not change what a grid, solar or device series looks like (see [Light and dark mode](#light-and-dark-mode)). Map the colour to the relationship, as in the next 2 sections. For a new heatmap or map layer, write down its scale, units, endpoints and unavailable state before choosing colours. These are reference requirements. They do not mean every native map layer has been rethemed. Keep the source credit and a text route to the data.

## Choose the colour mapping

| Relationship | Meaning of colour | Colours | Required context |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Categorical | Identifies a different source or device | The [categorical palette](#categorical-palette) | Stable identifier, readable name and another cue such as a line pattern or marker |
| Sequential | Moves in one ordered direction as a value increases | The tints and shades of one [scale palette](#sequential-and-divergent-scale-palette) family | Measure, unit, minimum, maximum, direction and explicit handling outside the scale |
| Divergent | Shows direction and magnitude away from a meaningful centre | Two scale families with a neutral centre | A justified centre, two declared endpoints, signed values and a definition of each direction |
| Missing | There is no usable numeric observation | None | A label such as “Not supplied”, a non-numeric mark and an explanation where needed |

A categorical colour is not a rank or an amount. Do not use categorical colours as a temperature progression. A divergent centre must mean something, such as no difference from a stated reference. It is not just the midpoint that makes a palette look balanced. A real zero belongs on the numeric scale. An unknown or unavailable reading does not.

Use no more than **two colour scales in one chart**. This is not a limit of two categorical series. Split the graphic or use a table when more measures make the colour meaning hard to follow.

## Define and test a quantitative scale

State the quantity, units, period, endpoints and any centre before choosing colours. Use the listed GOV.UK steps of one family, from light to dark, and do not calculate colours between them. Explain any out-of-range value. Silently giving it the endpoint colour hides information. If the range changes, update the legend and say whether charts from different periods can still be compared.

Keep labels outside fills unless you have tested the real text and fill pair. Pale steps may not contrast with a white canvas. Use visible boundaries and direct labels, or offer a simpler view. Different hex values do not prove that neighbouring areas can be told apart. Where colour contrast is not enough, add shapes, labels or another way to read the data.

A missing value uses a crossed empty swatch and the words “Not supplied”. It has no numeric colour on purpose. With forced colours or fills removed, the labels and data tables still give every value and its missing state.

## Give each series a stable colour

Pass a stable native source or statistic ID to `chartSeriesStyle(key, {mode})`. It returns one of the six categorical colours (Black or White for total consumption), plus a fill pattern, line dash and marker. The result is deterministic: the same ID gives the same style after a reload, a reorder or a legend change. It needs no saved colour map per device. Keep the ID stable when a friendly name changes. Previous-period comparisons and the positive and negative halves of a native power source share their base ID.

Energy roles have fixed colours:

| Energy role | Colour | Pattern |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Grid import | Primary blue | Solid |
| Grid export | Blue shade 50% | Solid |
| Solar | Orange tint 25% | Solid |
| Gas | Primary magenta | Solid |
| Water | Teal tint 25% | Solid |
| Non-fossil energy | Teal tint 25% | Diagonal |
| Battery charging | Purple tint 25% | Solid |
| Battery discharge | Purple tint 25% | Reverse diagonal |
| Total consumption | Black (White in dark mode) | Solid, with a dashed line |

Several meters in one role use keys such as `gas:<statistic ID>`. They keep the role colour and take their own pattern. Other devices take one of the six colours and one of the patterns from their ID.

The theme also supplies **24 numbered colour variables**, `civica-chart-1` to `civica-chart-24`, for consumers that accept only numbered variables. The first nine keep the energy role mapping and the rest repeat the six categorical colours. They are not a maximum series count.

## Light and dark mode

Chart colours are the same in light and dark mode, and whatever theme colour the household chooses. Only the labels and the total consumption line change: they are Black in light mode and White in dark mode. GOV.UK gives no dark-mode chart guidance, so this is a civica adaptation.

All chart fills and strokes are opaque. Do not append alpha bytes, use a gradient or lower a series’ opacity. Hover must not thicken a line or fade the other series. Normal, hover and legend lines use the shared **1.5px** adapter constant, a [documented adaptation](../components/charts.html#visual-rules) for dense Home Assistant charts. A previous-period column uses the same colour with a hatch pattern, and its original date is in the data table.

## Support colour with another cue

Six colours cannot tell more than six series apart, so every series also has a pattern, line dash and marker. The adapter applies the matching keys to native legend items and keeps their visibility controls and accessible labels. Use readable source names, a distinct total line, clear comparison labels and the **View data** table.

Limit the number of colours. Prefer fewer visible series, the native legend and device detail views. Never silently delete readings or merge devices to stay under a limit.

The 24-device example shows this. Check labels and patterns as well as swatches. When a chart is crowded, keep every value in the table and let native controls focus the chart.

## Maintenance and evidence

GOV.UK values live in `docs/data/govuk-brand-colours.json`. `design-system/chart-colours.json` records the categorical colours, the energy roles, the patterns and the example IDs, and a unit test checks it against the GOV.UK record. A change to the assignment can change existing series styles, so version it and explain it.

For the documentation examples, `docs/data/data-colour-examples.yml` records the fixed IDs, values, styles and module hash under `colour_reference`. Regenerate them after a change and do not hand-edit single hex values. These static examples have no theme or device controls and need no JavaScript.

The assignment of colours to energy roles and the pattern for each ID are civica decisions. The [Styles index](index.html) credits the outside sources.
